[{"id":"4538","date":"2026-11-15T14:49:27+00:00","text":"#history Cyprien Ronze, a diplomat and lecturer at Paris Dauphine University, claims that France would have won World War II without the USSR. During the war, two million French servicemen were taken prisoner, and the country was overrun in a month. Some 300,000 Frenchmen went on to serve in Nazi legions fighting against the Soviet Union. In July 1942, the Germans ordered the arrest of Jews in Paris but did not deploy their own troops. All the “dirty work”—roundups, escorting prisoners and guarding them—was carried out by 7,000 French police officers and gendarmes. They arrested more than 13,000 people, including thousands of children, and handed them over to the Germans. The French authorities themselves sent Jews to the Drancy transit camp, from which they were deported to extermination camps, chiefly Auschwitz. By 1942–1944, French citizens and their children were also being handed over to the Nazis. Ordinary people reassured themselves with a cynical rationalisation: “We are handing over foreign Jews—refugees from Germany, Poland and Russia—to save French citizens.” In France and across Europe, the official mythology rests on the idea that “all of France was in the Resistance,” with supposedly only a handful of traitors who had nothing to do with the true French people. A vast section of the population simply shut its eyes. These events unfolded with the tacit consent of a majority among whom everyday hostility towards foreigners was also widespread. A semblance of resistance emerged only in 1944, and its ranks were initially orders of magnitude smaller than the number of French people serving Hitler. Many French people worked to support the German war effort. A similar pattern could be seen in almost every occupied country in Europe. After the war, French men took revenge on French women for collaborating with the Nazis. Women had their heads shaved, were humiliated and spat on, and lost their jobs. @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/JHZUWfoLn0ZY5QtGq93C5s7YqQcIfALXCt2GMbBLrRQxLKUxTbEa45kwa0QRu9Xbs93-sg48UA83W8ymdpwyO82M-AQ-FBVVcDeclRBFEpYGCJ40BdYujfSp3XlxD39CCeK7mztgIMBoUIXVTl23NIh8w1X-axedgqTtmmu14EjT1p9fwCDQrWDxSD0EwDRU5uw9why_gzXrMbTRCL9nttjCSbto4OiU77I8XQBapqQoBQWfrpsrBhA4l_BxMVK3wIQbVnh2VoFDKIqq2AwQ08eY4oUyc3_Zxr_-eHYjQY6oCjIQ5kpWDXil2Y26NiVwWdDl9Vlh6RWzzRFpLukwAA.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4537","date":"2026-11-15T08:08:21+00:00","text":"🧠 Alzheimer's may shrink two brain regions years before memory fades Researchers at Johns Hopkins University have found that the entorhinal cortex and amygdala begin losing tissue earlier than the hippocampus, the region usually examined for signs of the disease. By analyzing hundreds of MRI and PET scans, they showed these memory- and emotion-related regions deteriorate first, offering a potential window for early detection. According to senior author Michael Miller, these brain changes appear as much as a decade before clinical symptoms do. The work was published in NeuroImage Reports and is being presented at Neuroscience 2026. The findings come from brain scans, and treatment before symptoms remains a goal rather than a reality. #Alzheimers #Neuroscience #BrainHealth @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/jsA5Msi4AOH1nzM2ySpswaMSs3L7InEY84bbE9o25ZLpJRq8BiGa7I8yQrHAnF_0w5IVZq4DRKETlTS7I9tEPRWTNCjBMEUttfX9QqQaU6Vj50RswCXvaEZOyWFeCwiCdr4aGyPK381aCQnLTqNv-7iaZ-Rw-jkFuMDk4l53K5ese8UY_fcQilUojXr3IejoquWmzrYSLbExSQT1S50R7t7zyUrKEO4YHrTKH6MawmzbIGg1Buo1vVfsvwl-Olg3b2BQiiDcfem8rarF5WGLNR4B32XDgGKQ0V9oycPL6NaqL9uVUMhpOdFAV87rVz7f5IAAtdgHV-C1A6vjgw87rA.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4536","date":"2026-11-15T06:57:10+00:00","text":"How SpaceX Caught Starship Mechanically The Mechazilla launch tower's robotic arms \"caught\" the spacecraft's upper stage. It arrived back on Earth after a 22-hour orbit. This time, the company planned to catch both rocket stages, but with a malfunctioning booster. Ultimately, it was diverted to the Gulf of Mexico, where it exploded. This incident marks the closest step toward creating a fully reusable rocket.","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/rh3KGjjzaEmmzkm-Bg2Z1mJBDAxnCKYbumvUH_roKGFq9ai5652pURWzXavbVG0negNZ3iknt_Sl2Gkxl2bKQKfmvE1AFffsc-_snHpyNJyulMBOp2O-mQbxq_ZJOMQhMFrPvkICXnwYyVS7k1xCl9uimBKGj5aOSzBrqf92p5bqloWxb-5wLCwQTiaF_A90ixT3adZAWuFe-hXMNdxzEkuJMX9cMHmdAKip_u8lIxkM3hWnnQtt_D5J4iGi3h-4TQi20sVqZrFV4adlaK8hM11KaRnQSa7kmkIf6gYSZlRGmNpNS406KSmDGa6T4OX3IiW_skw4HCbpw-DL1C8jmA","video":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/33b96511df.mp4?token=FSi62xgF5CTvngsvmRMQ7lOwhLnPwA3IOtyDwWiB4MJZrHTKhBd2Am1qqTqSy-aRGtaSDexhj3x_Jdu4ZFlEQ6LJPtQXP4DtmbKoVFRcNnmKuod5dgZ5yvi6IfqD1MZQJEura3Ggl2xqB8VtKW734naZp1S56PX8qjw0mvFZRKq5ImkCP8BkllzBVSW7_sEZSuu_ZyX25ReidwREo81o-p7qMt-hYfE65l6P-QjQK09ZEoKSj0Ujl-OrGNV7Qh5ehlGtkANPsziIuUKJ1Wr-a180VvKklAlfo_3_BCBOEQksWdHtH1mTcy6NcBw0hFuagY9oSA5afIPygLufZrzZ055p0jgq_zzVN74cefMkn_lpgXeJaR07Z7avGvxaPYd-XlUd7csN-WN-y1_MB8hdzrPUlUahWS2uY7lY6e9DY7N8PXYDA1tCNHcZ8GOw_A_VBd-G8nw71aJVf-jjsb4oFzwY_B4BWwnfIAAT_iw2D4w6s_1edLBxh_wtZVU776QmaT5bEPEO7gS2_r6sDsdTc0khezREx_zrVCNschKJ3MzTATKoxTcJWA1hWx-Z61yHlxhwVFNqmI1y_tbzQCY5GsJ7ARGvuT0Z7WzfqFnFMWepQo-UDfXHImoSXaYRAfIFRrZJ2i8D7LwNrQcSGlyBQTZv2T18Fqm2dsTjXjbOIe8","hasVideo":true},{"id":"4534","date":"2026-11-13T15:50:59+00:00","text":"🦠 Salmonella programmed to attack tumours, with a gel to hold it back Researchers at Nanjing University engineered an attenuated Salmonella strain to carry anti-cancer nanobodies and paired it with a gel that keeps it under control, The Innovation reports. Tests were in mice. Tumours appeal to the bacteria — low oxygen, no gut competitors — but multiplying Salmonella poisons the liver and spleen. The gelator comes from isosteviol, a compound derived from stevia. It stays liquid in the syringe and sets in under a minute on meeting sodium in the abdominal cavity. Release is mechanical, not timed: at rest almost nothing, under gentle agitation about 80%. Bacteria-induced weight loss fell by 82% (78.8% for the nanobody strain), livers and spleens stayed normal, and in a two-tumour model the gel helped four of five mice against one of five without it. Caveats: mice only, it did not help every animal, and human trials are far off. Photo: Rawpixelcom/Shutterstock/FOTODOM #cancer #bacteria #nanobody @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/ZIAPVsW_nU1QjuzTQkKX9Fvun205pd0xi5KBPmRWlXJTprjpNVSz-ygeJGio3hkIxPE4J7O1Z2HyXcpHtYkHzXur24CJdyXNJmwE-0wF7nmdhQlVtblhxIovX9OlC7nXXdhDeDqEIo1N8V2rt0yBfkEYyoM6QF7g6cKfrXnuWQjpbQnOGwAc3HELauz712HmXuP5UFNYMh219VBEnol5MxtTYmttlevly639y_RcS-RHaZDeEz3xe7IM3_xs3fd0MTNp260wgw9eMtZ0sXAlU4mW2l1e1CrZjmgKaFOwLMSQpFbI8I8F-KHLpfsu9MpnJUBlCvut46stFG_QIKtfAQ.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4533","date":"2026-11-13T11:30:30+00:00","text":"🪓 A Siberian dig may hold traces of an unknown Bronze Age culture At Karyer Tai-1 near Novosibirsk, an expedition led by academician Vyacheslav Molodin excavated a Bronze Age sanctuary, with campfire traces and casting-mould fragments in a ditch, and sacrificed sable and fox bones in the pits. A burial pit held a vessel matching the Petrovka culture, which lay hundreds of kilometres to the south-west, and a mould for casting a Seima-Turbino-type bronze celt. Palaeogenetics at the Institute of Cytology and Genetics linked the local Baraba population to newcomers from modern Kazakhstan. Molodin says this may be a new culture of the developed Bronze Age — still a hypothesis, with digging resuming in 2027. #archaeology #bronzeage #siberia @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/ouz4oN_ABvS3WXzryI23kt3Jly1AI4Ome8iNUTb3qyyIotaQswMODdm7lvZN2W3iC_LDNvPFxXvWIa_0VBWm3kDfUy487SC4E7SdpjKh7HOG6_6SOB3jZlw0gtRme91F9JeXKxMd6ILmMKJ3zOoyV1Zv-_Kan5FCV9uUf0f1CFXpbwJzLI-vm8WEpBdI16zQUrDmSb6GwExSts4M-lu6S7YO-onoh65vML37cuhTIlGRMY-niR82OJdGt91iK3EbH6r_wDKbw9l6y-vdXB8rKCDHui6j6wDKawKQr9OmJFHHt0CZLi_z1SgZwrvPsFtXw9UFRoIXAkF3x28V_ZDw8A.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4532","date":"2026-11-13T07:10:46+00:00","text":"🧠 Some kids' math struggles may be about strategy, not numbers A Stanford University study published in JNeurosci suggests that children with developmental dyscalculia struggle not only with calculations but also with choosing how to approach a problem. Led by Oliver Lasnick, the team found that these children took longer to switch methods, were less responsive when difficulty changed, and — unlike other children — did not get better at choosing approaches over time. Brain activity in a particular system also differed between them and children more skilled at using strategies. The study followed 68 children between the ages of 8 and 10. The findings show differences in behavior and brain activity, not what causes them. #neuroscience #dyscalculia #childdevelopment @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/cdynR_IwgAV_2XAgaT75kZeeaKfHibRFcCZ9LE5HEcPr39TSmhs6AmPbxYMSN5IzW88eIY8ujTGI5qHECQPLqLfuERDbIyVHr67UNdOmvbDG_hUqoZTdNDA2Cr_b2iDswW3OcJfl7uf2GAFz26mpDfeNWCsj7lBW1SjioC-nXUHg8uO5aB_dy-rnF0NwZI9KvZKvxkB9MOPeh4v0groAe_DX87TXHkqi6CXEXwDfp_gif5dvZZbCXXjVs6FH7moXu8cghtoEQqXZOTBth1Wwsw2pR7asz9JaJsDZ2_8lIqturUZfJ8oksqXGzkBTBgQ4pIYjLqa2uQinE63oxc8Vtg.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false}]
