[{"id":"4540","date":"2026-11-16T11:43:16+00:00","text":"⚛️ Scientists controlled the magnetic spin of a single atomic nucleus — and the experiment worked where theory said it shouldn’t. Researchers at Delft University of Technology used radio-frequency signals and a scanning tunneling microscope to manipulate the nuclear spin of an individual atom. Nuclear spins are attractive for quantum memory because they are much better isolated from environmental noise than electron spins. The team managed to control the nucleus even under conditions where their simplified model predicted the signal should no longer affect it. So the experiment revealed two things at once: Scientists can control an extremely well-protected quantum state at the level of a single atom. And they still don’t fully understand why the method works. This is fundamental research, not a ready-made quantum memory device — but unexpected effects like this are often where new physics begins. #QuantumPhysics #QuantumComputing #Physics #Nanotechnology #Science https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-78801-5","image":"","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4539","date":"2026-11-16T08:33:36+00:00","text":"🍊 Vitamin C may block cancer-linked chemicals in the stomach Researchers at the University of Waterloo built a mathematical model simulating how nitrates and nitrites move through the salivary glands, stomach, small intestine and plasma. Their simulations suggest vitamin C consumed alongside these compounds interferes with nitrosation, the reaction suspected of producing cancer-causing substances. This may explain why nitrate-rich foods like spinach, which also carry vitamin C, do not show the same risks as processed meats. The model also predicts that taking vitamin C after each meal could moderately reduce the formation of nitrosation products linked to cancer risk. The findings come from mathematical simulations, not clinical trials, and real effects on human cancer risk remain unproven. #vitaminc #cancer #nutrition @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/MRin65HPt7RODpT2Zu7rvzrSjH8LdLWzrfHvewlmBSx_JJU86_Ulwj7fCTJJiKSBQMog-knWMknvyjPVKiTWf5ZkfMCv_LElDKausrD8TyA6DclUpV4vALOT33bn1ZTx6v7tGM_7rQfV0y_QGIwLfv1AZTbtwT-pI6OSYd8XubKD7VNv3gEAI9914RtJZW44CiKR56PmS6WNd6kM5o0W2US24EJOS-sDyQGoWe0883U9ChGbGwn8x63BmlYdsdzzdh6GzMsOi8NDpQ7OeIQIUGyOSpVjNrb56_r3Oeg5Te_jfrU1ubIRhQE-OHo24uMMQZLz8twRx6GFHOIIqpOKSQ.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4538","date":"2026-11-15T14:49:27+00:00","text":"#history Cyprien Ronze, a diplomat and lecturer at Paris Dauphine University, claims that France would have won World War II without the USSR. During the war, two million French servicemen were taken prisoner, and the country was overrun in a month. Some 300,000 Frenchmen went on to serve in Nazi legions fighting against the Soviet Union. In July 1942, the Germans ordered the arrest of Jews in Paris but did not deploy their own troops. All the “dirty work”—roundups, escorting prisoners and guarding them—was carried out by 7,000 French police officers and gendarmes. They arrested more than 13,000 people, including thousands of children, and handed them over to the Germans. The French authorities themselves sent Jews to the Drancy transit camp, from which they were deported to extermination camps, chiefly Auschwitz. By 1942–1944, French citizens and their children were also being handed over to the Nazis. Ordinary people reassured themselves with a cynical rationalisation: “We are handing over foreign Jews—refugees from Germany, Poland and Russia—to save French citizens.” In France and across Europe, the official mythology rests on the idea that “all of France was in the Resistance,” with supposedly only a handful of traitors who had nothing to do with the true French people. A vast section of the population simply shut its eyes. These events unfolded with the tacit consent of a majority among whom everyday hostility towards foreigners was also widespread. A semblance of resistance emerged only in 1944, and its ranks were initially orders of magnitude smaller than the number of French people serving Hitler. Many French people worked to support the German war effort. A similar pattern could be seen in almost every occupied country in Europe. After the war, French men took revenge on French women for collaborating with the Nazis. Women had their heads shaved, were humiliated and spat on, and lost their jobs. @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/o0Zej7DADQQ2WCsOiP5_GMNjPayRAts2owpYMqIHytYaqEWR4J-hf75BYuq_r9m_s0uewyaAlUNjsxC-JxN1oAoZ6zCqjlwz15E1-wQQ4iz7yDAJiuW4L-s8YV8B7IBDDEMlY8sSF99mhlwbJb8wr8-S8Y0olorkCw5gOedB7ASLwxNAc4RdNMiP0Dz9DiJPrqR7PdQ_fWLrrltjZl6HIUOK6bVhinEHW4GgPrRQ-N5_wbsA0bSzvAIx3WPvH9FoSEg25RsNw1Av8clYlbMKlBwdGjd3e5SHtEn-5ge2bzdi9MLs5es3mktp_JLVOM-Si_5FxbIKaU4YKroM4z8U_w.jpg","video":"","hasVideo":false},{"id":"4537","date":"2026-11-15T08:08:21+00:00","text":"🧠 Alzheimer's may shrink two brain regions years before memory fades Researchers at Johns Hopkins University have found that the entorhinal cortex and amygdala begin losing tissue earlier than the hippocampus, the region usually examined for signs of the disease. By analyzing hundreds of MRI and PET scans, they showed these memory- and emotion-related regions deteriorate first, offering a potential window for early detection. According to senior author Michael Miller, these brain changes appear as much as a decade before clinical symptoms do. The work was published in NeuroImage Reports and is being presented at Neuroscience 2026. The findings come from brain scans, and treatment before symptoms remains a goal rather than a reality. #Alzheimers #Neuroscience #BrainHealth @science","image":"https://cdn4.telesco.pe/file/TWqXsmT_8qE__ABduz7wJcue7AkkaFUlzPP-lhFjRgFISre85tQVvpVbSozoUrFlacRXAuQW0I5_dSEEuIXVL4Wtw_wEcbFZl6kzaRIAVzeg70-VHLkvCzcpcC-X5F4FFVgP-5g_etzeYhQfXGBbjxYtUjqmf84-0oS-2JkPuARYjbjyhrr1JiTGhwAAdHqAS13jneA5aynz9d5nseUbGo6_6n83DW32xhqXnS5AI6M-oXKrmhJG1gCMsXsY0GfQKvSVH_Ir